must not contain d0. So minimal failure set is required_failure_items = [d4,d7] (since d0 is not needed and must be absent). But if d0 is absent, then the set [d4,d7] would cause failure. So minimal_failure = [\"d4\",\"d7\"]. For case 2, outcome \"failure\", tests? We need to compute number of tests performed by delta debugging algorithm.\n\nBut what is the exact algorithm? There is also the \"items\" list; the oracle is defined by the payload: a set S (subset of items) fails iff (required_failure_items subset of S) and (unresolved_without intersection S is empty). That is plausible.\n\nNow, we need to implement ddmin that finds a minimal subset of items that still causes failure. The minimal subset is a set T such that T fails, and no proper subset of T fails. For the given oracle, the minimal failure set is exactly required_failure_items (because any superset that includes required_failure_items and excludes unresolved_without will also fail; but to be minimal, we need to remove items not in required_failure_items). Since required_failure_items is already minimal in the sense that removing any of them would cause failure to disappear (because then not all required are present). So the minimal failure set is required_failure_items. But wait: what if required_failure_items contains an item that is also in unresolved_without? That would be contradictory because a set must contain required and not contain unresolved; an item cannot be both. So the payload is consistent.\n\nThus minimal_failure = required_failure_items (as a list). outcome = \"failure\". tests = number of test executions performed by the delta debugging algorithm to reach that conclusion, starting from the full set.\n\nThe algorithm: ddmin(C) where C is the full set of items. It tests subsets. The standard ddmin algorithm (as in the paper) is:\n\ndef ddmin(cfg):\n    # cfg is the set of items that cause failure initially.\n    # Returns a 1-minimal subset.\n    n = 2\n    while len(cfg) >= 2:\n        # split into n subsets\n        subsets = split(cfg, n)\n        # test complement of each subset? Actually algorithm: test each subset.\n        # If any subset fails, then we can reduce cfg to that subset and continue.\n        # If no subset fails, try complements: test each complement; if a complement passes, we can remove that subset.\n        # If neither, increase n.\n        # Standard ddmin: \n        # for each subset:\n        #   if test(subset) == FAIL:\n        #       cfg = subset\n        #       n = 2\n        #       break\n        # else:\n        #   for each complement:\n        #       if test(complement) == PASS:\n        #           cfg = complement\n        #           n = 2\n        #           break\n        #   else:\n        #       n = min(n*2, len(cfg))\n        # return cfg\n    return cfg\n\nBut the exact implementation may vary. Also the test oracle is as defined: test(subset) returns FAIL if subset contains all required_failure_items and contains no unresolved_without, else PASS.\n\nGiven that the minimal set is required_failure_items, the number of tests will depend on the order of items and the splitting. For case 0: items = 8 items, required = [d1,d4], unresolved = []. The algorithm will try to reduce. Since the failure condition is simply having both d1 and d4, any subset that contains both will fail. The algorithm will try splitting into 2 subsets. Standard ddmin splits into roughly equal halves. The initial set is all 8 items. It will test subset1 and subset2. If either subset contains both d1 and d4, it will fail and become the new cfg. If not, it tests complements. In this case, it's likely that one of the halves contains both d1 and d4? It depends on ordering. The items are ordered as given: d0,d1,d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7. That's the list order. Splitting into n=2 gives first half [d0,d1,d2,d3] and second half [d4,d5,d6,d7]. The first half contains d1 but not d4 -> test pass. Second half contains d4 but not d1 -> test pass. So no